\myparagraph{Quasimetrics} are a generalization of metrics in that they do not require symmetry. For a set $\mathcal{X}$, a \qmet is a function $d \colon \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X} \rightarrow \R_{\geq 0}$ such that \begin{align}
    \forall x_1, x_2, x_3,& \quad d(x_1, x_2) + d(x_2, x_3) \geq d(x_1, x_3) \\[-0.025cm]
    \forall x, & \quad d(x, x) = 0.
\end{align}
We use $\mqmet(\mathcal{X})$ to denote all such quasimetrics over $\mathcal{X}$.

\Cref{} shows that $-V^* \in \mqmet(\mathcal{S})$. In fact, the other direction also holds: for any $d \in \mqmet(\mathcal{S})$, $-d$ is the optimal value function for some MDP defined on $\mathcal{S}$. 

