In this work we study the notion of a Whittaker module in the setting of generalized Weyl algebras.  Generalized Weyl algebras were introduced by  Bavula \cite{} and have been studied  extensively since then (see for example, \cite{}, \cite{}, \cite{}).  We shall use the definition of a generalized Weyl algebra (GWA) given in \cite[1.1]{}.    


Suppose that $R$ is a unital associative algebra over a field  $\mathbb{F}$ with $\phi = (\phi_i)_{i \in \mathcal I}$ a collection of pairwise commuting automorphisms of $R$ indexed by the set $\mathcal I$ (which may be finite or infinite), and let $t = (t_i)_{i \in \mathcal I}$ be a collection of nonzero central elements of $R$ also indexed by $\mathcal I$.    The \emph{generalized Weyl algebra} $A = R(\phi, t)$ with base ring $R$ is the associative algebra generated over $R$ by elements $X_i$ and $Y_i$  for $i \in \mathcal I$  with defining relations 
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{} Y_iX_i=t_i &\hspace{.4in}& X_iY_i= \phi_i(t_i) \\
\label{} X_ir = \phi_i(r) X_i &\hspace{.4in}& Y_i\phi_i(r) = r Y_i
\end{eqnarray}
for $r \in R$,   and 
\begin{equation}\label{}
[X_i,X_j] = [Y_i,Y_j] = [X_i,Y_j] = 0
\end{equation}
for $i \neq j$, where $[\ ,\ ]$ denotes the commutator $[a,b] = ab-ba$. 

\textbf{We always assume that the algebra $R$ is a domain which is left Noetherian}.  Thus by \cite[Prop.~1.3]{}, the algebra $A = R(\phi, t)$ is a domain,  
and $A$ is left Noetherian if $\mathcal I$ is finite.   
