We investigate in which cases continuous structure learning approaches can and cannot perform well and why this happens. We focus on several specifications of the data and search procedure, including varsortability, data standardization, nonconvexity, thresholding, and sparsity. Despite the simplicity of continuous structure learning approaches, we demonstrate that they may suffer from various technical issues discussed below. Our goal here is not to resolve all these issues, but rather to analyze and make them transparent, as well as suggesting possible directions. We hope that our studies could stimulate future works on developing more reliable continuous structure learning approaches, e.g., to overcome the nonconvexity and thresholding issues. Detailed discussions are provided below.

