The optimal cluster of participants significantly varies along with the runtime variance. Figure~\ref{FL-runtime-variance}(a) compares the achieved energy efficiency over eight different combinations of participant devices ({\tt C0} to {\tt C7} defined in Table 4 of Section~\ref{}) in the absence of on-device interference and with stable network signal strength. In such an ideal execution environment, the most energy efficient cluster is {\tt C3}, achieving 3.2x higher energy efficiency than the baseline {\tt C0}. In the presence of on-device interference, the optimal cluster becomes {\tt C1} (Figure~\ref{FL-runtime-variance}(b)), whereas when the network signal strength is weak, the optimal cluster switches to {\tt C5} (Figure~\ref{FL-runtime-variance}(c)).

Intuitively, in the presence of on-device interference, it is more beneficial to select high-end devices to participate in FL---this is when the higher power consumption from the additional compute and memory capacities on an high-end device becomes justifiable. On the other hand, when the network signal strength is poor, FL becomes communication bound. Thus, selecting mid- and low-end devices leads to higher energy efficiency. While low power devices bring the energy efficiency advantage, it is at the expense of increased training time per model aggregation round, leading to potentially longer time-to-convergence. 

