\section{Learning with Unbiased Label Noises}
In this section, we remodel the problem of deep learning under the Stochastic Gradient Least Square estimator with Unbiased Label Noises (SGD-ULN) settings, where we are specifically interested in the induced bias and the effects of implicit regularization to the generalization performances.

\begin{definition}[Learning with Unbiased Label Noises]
A set of vectors $\mathcal{D}=\{x_1,x_2,x_3,\dots,x_N\}$ drawn from the random variable $\mathcal{X}$ and a $d$-dimensional vector $\theta^*\in \mathbb{R}^d$ are given. The response $y_i$ of the neural network $f^*(x)$ to each sample $x_i\in\mathcal{D}$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
    y_i=y_i^*+\varepsilon_i,\ y_i^*=f^*(x_i),\ \mathrm{ and }\  

    \mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_i] =0,\ \mathrm{var}[\varepsilon_i]=\sigma^2_i.
\end{equation}
The problem of learning is to estimate a $d$-dimensional vector $\widehat\theta\in\mathbb{R}^d$, which beats the true neural network $f^*(x)$, using the labeled pairs $\{(x_1,y_1),\dots,(x_N,y_N)\}$.
\end{definition}

